Kotlin/Android: my coroutine leaks and the UI freezes. What is wrong?
lifecycleScope.launch {
  while (true) {
    val data = api.fetch()
    delay(1000)
    textView.text = data
  }
}

Reasoning: lifecycleScope cancels on destroy, so the leak is not the scope; the freeze is likely api.fetch() blocking the main thread. lifecycleScope.launch defaults to Main dispatcher. Also while(true) polling is wasteful.

Fix: move IO to Dispatchers.IO, collect on main, and stop with a proper trigger:
``kotlin
lifecycleScope.launch {
  flow { while (currentCoroutineContext().isActive) { emit(api.fetch()); delay(1000) } }
    .flowOn(Dispatchers.IO)
    .collect { textView.text = it }
}
``

If api.fetch() is a blocking Retrofit call, wrap with withContext(Dispatchers.IO). If it is suspend, it already moves off main only if implemented correctly. Check for runBlocking.

Reasoning: regular files usually accept the whole buffer because the page cache absorbs it (barring ENOSPC, signals, or RLIMIT_FSIZE). Sockets have a bounded send buffer; a non-blocking or signal-interrupted write can accept partially. Partial writes are always legal for both.

Correct pattern:
``c
size_t off = 0;
while (off < n) {
  ssize_t w = write(fd, buf + off, n - off);
  if (w < 0) { if (errno == EINTR) continue; perror("write"); break; }
  off += (size_t)w;
}
``

For sockets, also consider SIGPIPE (use MSG_NOSIGNAL or ignore SIGPIPE) and EAGAIN for non-blocking descriptors.

